WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:05.960
Christian Bale steals the show as Gorr the
God Butcher in "Thor 4," but what's the story

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.130
behind his deadly Necrosword?

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.929
And what does it mean for the future of the
MCU?

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:12.679
Keep watching to find out.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.469
"All gods will die."

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:19.570
The Marvel Cinematic Universe is packed with
iconic weapons and costumes used by the countless

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:23.810
heroes and villains across the multiverse,
and it is committed to expanding that armory

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.589
in "Thor: Love and Thunder."

00:00:25.589 --> 00:00:30.289
In the sequel from "Thor: Ragnarok" director
Taika Waititi, Thor heads off on a quest to

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:35.230
rediscover himself after the events of "Avengers:
Endgame," and he winds up crossing paths with

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.800
Gorr the God Butcher in the process.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:43.660
In late June, Gorr actor Bale told Total Film
he was concerned about Gorr's minimal costume

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:47.379
in the comics, telling the outlet,
"I thought, 'They don't have the right man

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:48.379
for that!'

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:52.039
And then Taika quickly dispelled any notions
of running around in that.

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:55.489
But I always did think what he could do with
this in front of a bluescreen — he could

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:57.879
chuck on whatever he wants later on."

00:00:57.879 --> 00:01:02.059
Thankfully, the live-action version of Gorr
is a little more covered up, and the film

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:05.500
does give the villain his iconic weapon: the
Necrosword.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:10.980
Gorr uses the jet-black weapon with a devastating
fury in the highly anticipated sequel, especially

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.700
in a brutal fight with Thor, Jane, and Valkyrie
in the Necroverse.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:20.510
But the origins of the Necrosword in the MCU
are just as murky as the blade itself.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:24.720
The object has a fascinating history in the
comics — and it could mean that a huge villain

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.460
is somewhere on the horizon.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:31.470
As far as the MCU is concerned, "Thor: Love
and Thunder" never actually confirms who the

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:34.330
Necrosword belongs to before Gorr gains control
of it.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.880
Instead, Thor explains it's been passed down
since the dawn of time, and it corrupts anyone

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.360
who uses it, infecting them with darkness
until they die.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.450
When Gorr first gets his hands on it, it's
next to a dead knight wearing a bulky set

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.890
of black armor who's clearly succumbed to
its influence.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.821
In the comics, Gorr's Necrosword belongs to
Knull the Symbiote God before the "Thor" villain

00:01:54.821 --> 00:01:56.550
gets his claws on it.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:01.850
In Donny Cates' "Venom" #4, the villain recounts
his origin to Eddie Brock, explaining that

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:06.520
billions of years in the past, he lived alone
in a void that quickly became the battleground

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.619
for a Celestial war.

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:13.099
Because they invade Knull's empty kingdom,
he conjures a sword out of the void and beheads

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:17.460
a Celestial before turning its severed head
into a forge, where he makes the Necrosword

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.090
a permanent weapon.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.569
Essentially, it's a blade of living darkness
that also traps the soul of anything it kills

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:24.959
inside it.

00:02:24.959 --> 00:02:29.480
Because Knull is a key part of the Venom mythology,
it seems likely that Sony has the rights to

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.599
use Knull however it sees fit, even though
the studio bought the rights to Spider-Man

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:38.040
and his related characters a number of years
before the villain debuted in the comics.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.349
As such, this is probably why co-writers Taika
Waititi and Jennifer Kaytin Robinson use an

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:47.109
ambiguous figure rather than acknowledging
the Knull of it all.

00:02:47.109 --> 00:02:50.920
It's unclear if the two studios could (or
would) strike a deal for Knull to appear in

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.170
the MCU, but it seems far more likely that
he'd show up in a future "Venom" film with

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.530
Tom Hardy's symbiotic antihero first.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:04.379
"All of us should be able to live together
upon this ball of rock!"

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:08.510
It's also worth pointing out that the Necrosword
is also called the All-Black, and it's actually

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.319
the very first Symbiote.

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:12.840
Yes, the sword is technically alive.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:17.379
What's interesting is that it also cloaks
Knull in a set of black armor with a red insignia

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:20.890
on the front, which gives the immortal villain
a terrifying look.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:25.329
So the dead knight in the opening of "Thor:
Love and Thunder" clearly a nod to the Necrosword's

00:03:25.329 --> 00:03:30.709
origins, without getting into the messy implications
of Knull himself being in the MCU.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:34.999
Gorr briefly meets Knull in "Thor: God of
Thunder" #6, where the villain reveals his

00:03:34.999 --> 00:03:37.360
origin to an imprisoned Volstagg.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.890
The issue explains that Gorr is exiled from
his tribe for his rage against the gods, and

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:46.560
shortly after he stumbles on an armored Knull
fighting a golden god in the desert — although

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.139
their fight has ended in a stalemate because
they've impaled each other.

00:03:50.139 --> 00:03:54.299
All-Black quickly jumps into Gorr's hand as
a dagger, and he uses it to kill the golden

00:03:54.299 --> 00:03:59.249
god in revenge for the deaths of his mother,
his wife, and his daughter over the years.

00:03:59.249 --> 00:04:03.279
That's not too far from the film's opening,
where Gorr stumbles into Rapu's realm and

00:04:03.279 --> 00:04:05.999
uses the discarded weapon to kill his former
idol.

00:04:05.999 --> 00:04:10.609
The Necrosword's symbiote abilities are a
huge part of the film too, as Gorr brings

00:04:10.609 --> 00:04:15.500
monstrous Black Berserkers out of the abyss
inside the Necrosword to plague the Asgardians

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.780
and steal the children of New Asgard.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:22.640
When the villain kills several gods in "Thor:
God of Thunder" #1 and #3, he leaves a few

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:26.910
Berserkers behind as sentries to slaughter
anyone investigating their deaths.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.900
Taika Waititi clearly did his homework.

